All three scenarios hold, so I wrote `{"findings": []}` to `findings.json`. I didn't run any build or test command. Every result below comes from reading the code and working each row through it by hand.

- **Scope coverage cases:** `osq_core::scope_covers_path` is in `crates/core/src/scope/glob.rs`. It is the old kernel/specs matcher moved over unchanged, and the old one now just calls it. Each of the 11 rows gives the expected result. For example, `?` won't match `/`, so `src//.ts` is not covered. A scope ending in `/` gets `**` added, and `src\a.ts` is converted to `src/a.ts`. The `scope_coverage_cases` test in `crates/core/tests/scope_audit.rs` asserts every row.
- **Scope audit cases:** I checked all 19 rows against `audit_scope` in `crates/core/src/scope/audit.rs`, including:
  - lock files follow a covered manifest at or below their own folder;
  - frozen-test checks ignore added files;
  - `001-ab` does not count as inside `001-a`;
  - results are sorted by path, so `Cargo.lock` comes before `crates/beta/Cargo.toml`;
  - "outside scope" comes before "frozen test" for the same path.

  The `scope_audit_cases` test asserts the exact disallowed list for every row.
- **Lock file at the root follows any covered manifest:** the root `Cargo.lock` passes because the root folder accepts a manifest in any folder. The test of the same name asserts that nothing is disallowed.
- **Would the tests pass without the change?** No. Before this change `osq_core` had no `scope_covers_path`, `audit_scope` or related types, so the new tests would not even compile.

I agree with the executor's claims, including the extra edge-case assertions it says it added beyond the scenario tables.
